<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- SpotBugs project decisions: a rule, or a rule in part of the code, that the team has decided not
     to enforce. One Match per decision, with a comment giving the reason, for example:

       <!- - JPA entities share associations by design (reason ...) - ->
       <Match><Package name="~team\.projectpulse\..*"/><Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/></Match>

     Unlike the baseline, this file may grow, but every entry is a reviewed decision. -->
<FindBugsFilter>
    <!-- D1, turned off: EI_EXPOSE_REP and EI_EXPOSE_REP2. All 219 baselined findings fall in four kinds of class, and
         none of them owns an invariant a shared reference could break:
         1. JPA @Entity classes, and MyUserPrincipal, which wraps one for Spring Security. Their associations must be
            the same objects Hibernate manages: a defensive copy of a @OneToMany list detaches it from dirty checking
            and orphanRemoval, and a copied @ManyToOne is a second, unmanaged instance of a row.
         2. Spring singletons (@RestController, @Service, @Component) holding constructor-injected beans, which are
            shared by design.
         3. DTOs and records built per request by a converter or service, serialized by Jackson, and discarded.
         4. DocumentTemplate, loaded once from YAML by DocumentTemplateRegistry. Its only two callers,
            DocumentService.applyTemplate and DataInitializer.applyTemplate, iterate getSections() and never modify it.
         Check: every class named in the removed baseline entries is one of the four. -->
    <Match><Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/></Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
